BspTree: parse the node tree iteratively, so its depth is not the call stack.

`_parse_bsp_nodes` recurses once per set flag bit in the node it just read:

	if ((flags & 0x01) != 0) {
		node.front_index = ...;
		_parse_bsp_nodes(in, nodes, indices, version, self_index, flags & 0x04);
	}

Nothing bounds how often that can happen. A node is 49 bytes on the wire (a
bbox, two counts, the flags byte and the split plane), so a file that sets bit 0
on every node descends one level per 49 bytes it carries -- and a BSP tree is
part of a world file, where 49 bytes are nothing. **Measured on the fixture
grown to a chain of 100,000 nodes (4.9 MB): the process dies with 0xC00000FD,
a stack overflow, which is not catchable and takes the whole process with it.**
That is worse in the editor than the number suggests: node's main thread has an
8 MB stack, and worlds are loaded on a `worker_threads` worker, whose default is
half of it.

This is the site `docs/plans/level-editor.md` 16.11 has named as open since the
fuzzing began, and the one the fuzzer could not reach: 200 seeds of 20 byte
writes over a 4 KB fixture cannot manufacture a chain that needs megabytes.

The fix is not a depth limit. Every bound in this series so far checks a count
against the bytes left in the reader, but that argument does not work here --
the depth a *valid* world may reach rises with its size, and a real BSP is
built by ZenGin's own compiler with no documented ceiling. Guessing one risks
refusing a world the engine loads. Parsing the tree iteratively removes the
question instead: the walk keeps its own stack of the back children still owed,
so depth costs heap, which the reader already bounds elsewhere, and not the
call stack, which nothing can.

The traversal is unchanged and deliberately so -- the entry stream is read
positionally, so the order of the reads *is* the format. A node is read, then
its whole front subtree, then its back subtree; `front_index` is assigned as
the front child begins and `back_index` as the back child begins, after the
front subtree has been read.

It also fixes a latent use-after-realloc that only the recursion could have.
`node` is a reference into `nodes`, and the recursive version assigned
`node.back_index` *after* the front subtree had pushed its own nodes onto that
same vector. `BspTree::load` calls `nodes.reserve(node_count)` with the file's
own count first, which is why this has never been seen -- a valid file's tree
fits the reservation it declares. A file whose `node_count` is smaller than the
nodes it actually carries reallocates mid-walk and the assignment writes into
freed memory. The iterative walk indexes the vector instead.

Upstreamable as-is: no API change, no behaviour change on any valid world, and
the transformation is mechanical.

